mode: "100644"
text: "# The Three Gates\n\nTrend-jacking works only when a trend clears **all three** gates — FIT, SAFETY, and TIMING. Most\nbrand trend-jacking fails because someone skipped a gate: they forced an irrelevant trend (fit),\ntouched a sensitive moment (safety), or arrived late (timing). Run every trend through all three, in\nthis order, before any execution. **Any single fail = don't do it.**\n\nThe order matters: check FIT and SAFETY before TIMING, because a fast no is better than a fast\nmistake. Speed never overrides fit or safety.\n\n---\n\n## Gate 1 — FIT (is this *your* trend?)\n\nThe question: **can you add a genuine angle, or are you just slapping your logo on someone else's\nmoment?**\n\nPass if:\n- There's an **authentic connection** to your brand, product, niche, or audience.\n- You can bring a **real perspective** — your take, your world, your value — not just participation.\n- Your **audience would actually enjoy** you doing it (it fits how they see you).\n\nFail (skip it) if:\n- The only reason to do it is \"it's popular.\"\n- You'd have to contort the trend to make it relate.\n- It clashes with your brand identity or would confuse your audience.\n\n**Not every trend is your trend.** A B2B accounting tool doesn't need to do a teen dance. The best\ntrend-jacks feel inevitable in hindsight because the fit was real.\n\n---\n\n## Gate 2 — SAFETY (should anyone touch this?)\n\nThe question: **would a brand inserting itself here look opportunistic, tone-deaf, or\nexploitative?** This is the gate that protects the brand from getting dragged.\n\n**Hard no — do not trend-jack:**\n- **Tragedies, disasters, deaths, violence, crises, illness.** Marketing on top of human suffering\n  reads as exploitation, full stop. (A genuine, non-promotional response — real help, a donation,\n  staying silent — is different from a trend-jack.)\n- **Politically or socially divisive moments** ridden for attention. Inserting a brand to farm reach\n  off controversy is a brand-safety landmine.\n- **Anything mocking or punching down** at a group, or built on a stereotype.\n- **Sensitive cultural/religious moments** treated flippantly.\n- A trend whose **origin is harmful/misleading** (misinformation, a harmful challenge).\n\n**Proceed with caution (judgment call):**\n- Edgy humor — fine if it fits the brand and punches up/sideways, risky otherwise.\n- A trend with murky origins — verify before joining.\n\nWhen in doubt, **sit it out.** The reach from a risky trend is never worth the reputational damage,\nand \"we were just joining the trend\" is not a defense anyone accepts.\n\n---\n\n## Gate 3 — TIMING (is the window open?)\n\nTrends have a lifecycle: **emerging → rising → peak → saturated → dead.** The window to ride one is\non the way **up**.\n\n- **Emerging/rising → GO.** Early is where the reach is; you look in-the-know.\n- **Peak → maybe**, if you can ship *now* and add something fresh.\n- **Saturated/dead → skip.** Jumping on a played-out trend reads as late and out-of-touch — worse\n  than not doing it.\n\nTrends decay fast (a sound can be over in days). So once FIT and SAFETY pass, **speed matters** —\nbut speed is the *third* gate, not the first. A fast post on a trend that fails fit or safety is just\na fast problem.\n\n---\n\n## The one-line test\n\nBefore executing, you should be able to say: **\"This trend genuinely fits us (fit), it's safe and\nnon-exploitative to touch (safety), and it's still on the way up (timing).\"** If you can't say all\nthree, don't post it.\n"
